course: dictionary
lesson: 10852
title: Leftover traces in science words
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Some words come from science, and they describe things we can see inside living creatures. Vestigial is one of them. It describes a body part that is still there but has become small or useless over a very long time. «The tailbone, or coccyx, is what is left of a tail, and it still anchors muscles; such leftover parts are called vestigial, and they may have new jobs.» The tailbone is still there, but it is a leftover trace of something bigger.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Leftover» and «what is left of» tell you that vestigial means remaining from something that used to be bigger. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence: «such leftover parts are called small leftover traces» still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: The same word can be used outside science. Vestigial can mean a small trace of something that used to be more important. A word can change its feel when it moves from a science lesson into everyday talk, so check the clues each time.
fact: Vestigial is an adjective, which means it describes a noun, like a vestigial organ or vestigial wings.
fact: «Vestigial» means still there, but small or useless, after a very long time.
fact: The tailbone, or coccyx, is an example of a vestigial body part in humans.
fact: Ostriches and kiwis have wings that cannot fly, and science calls such leftover wings vestigial.
fact: Some whales have tiny leg bones, which are vestigial. Vestigial parts are clues that living things changed over a long time.
exercise: «The tailbone, or coccyx, is what is left of a tail, and it still anchors muscles; such leftover parts are called vestigial.» What does vestigial mean here? | Brand new and growing fast | Very loud and noisy | Full of bright light | *Left over as a small trace of something that used to be bigger
exercise: Ostriches and kiwis have wings that cannot fly. Which word does science use for wings like these? | Famished | Luminous | *Vestigial | Curt
exercise: Find and copy the word that means a small leftover trace in: «Some whales have tiny leg bones, a vestigial trace of legs.» | whales | legs | *vestigial | tiny
test: In science, which body part in humans is an example of a vestigial organ? | Heart | *Tailbone | Lungs | Eyes
test: Vestigial means the same as which phrase? | Full of noise | Very hungry | Brand new and shiny | *Small and useless, left over from long ago
exam: Which sentence uses vestigial correctly? | *Kiwis have vestigial wings that they cannot use to fly | Ella ate a vestigial sandwich for lunch | The brand-new bridge was vestigial | The vestigial sun is shining over the park
